The Problem: The "Heavy" Dependency
Imagine you are testing a UserService. To do its job, the UserService needs to talk to
an Oracle Database via a UserRepository.

If you use pure JUnit, you have to physically boot up an Oracle Database, insert a user,
run the test, and delete the user. Your test will take 5 seconds to run. If you have
2,000 tests, your build takes 3 hours. Furthermore, if the Wi-Fi drops, your test fails
even though your Java code is perfect!

The Senior Solution: Mocking
Mockito allows you to create a "hollow clone" (a Mock) of the UserRepository.
This clone doesn't connect to a database. It doesn't even have real code inside it.
You simply "train" the clone by saying: "If the UserService asks you for User #1, just
instantly return the word 'Mahesh'."

Because there is no real database, the test runs in 1 millisecond and never fails
due to Wi-Fi.
/////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
Step 1: The Dependency (The Database)
        enterprise apps, we usually communicate with databases through interfaces. We
        don't to test this interface today; we just want to bypass it.
Step 2: The Class Under Test (The Business Logic)
        This is the class we actually want to test. Notice how it requires a
        UserRepository to function.
Step 3: The Mockito Magic
        Go to your src/test/java folder and create this test class.
        Pay close attention to the three magical Mockito annotations: @ExtendWith,
        @Mock, and @InjectMocks.

The Parameterized Test (JUnit 5)
To get the exact test runner output you provided (User age X should get Y discount),
you can use JUnit 5's @ParameterizedTest with a custom name attribute.

Key Components
* @ParameterizedTest: Runs the same test method multiple times using different inputs,
  which is much cleaner than writing five separate test methods.
* name = "...": This attribute customizes the display name in your IDE's test runner
  (like IntelliJ or Eclipse), producing the exact User age 10 should get 0.50 discount
  format in the console tree.
* @CsvSource: Injects the comma-separated strings directly into the age and
  expectedDiscount parameters.
///////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
The Problem: Void Methods and Hidden Actions
In the previous test, getUserGreeting() returned a String, so you used assertEquals to
check if the String was correct.
But what if a method returns void?
Imagine a UserRegistration class. When a user registers, it saves them to the database
and then sends a Welcome Email. It doesn't return anything. How do you test if the
email was actually sent without actually spamming real email addresses?

The Senior Solution: Mockito verify()
We create a Mock of the EmailService. We run the registration logic. Then, we ask
Mockito: "Hey, did the UserService actually press the 'Send Email' button on the fake
Email Service? How many times did it press it? What message did it try to send?"

Step 1: The Dependency (The Email Server)
Step 2: The Class Under Test
Step 3: The Verification Test

How to intentionally break this test to see it work:
1. Run the test as-is. It will pass.
2. Go into RegistrationService.java and comment out the emailService.sendEmail(...) line.
3. Run the test again.
   Mockito will crash the test with a brilliant error message:
   Wanted but not invoked: mockEmailService.sendEmail(...)
   Actually, there were zero interactions with this mock.

You have now mastered the two pillars of Mockito:
1. when().thenReturn(): Used to fake incoming data from a database.
2. verify(): Used to prove that outgoing actions (like saving or emailing) actually
   happened.
///////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
The Argument Captor.
You know how to use verify() when you know exactly what string or number is going to be
passed to the mock. But what happens when the class you are testing creates a complex,
dynamic object internally and sends that object to the database?

The Problem: The Hidden Object
Imagine you are building a Security System. Every time a user logs in, the
SecurityService creates an AuditLog object to record the event and saves it to the
database.

The Issue: Because the AuditLog object is instantiated inside the login() method, your
test file has no access to it. You cannot use verify(mock).save(exactObject) because
you don't have the object to compare it against!

The Senior Solution: Mockito @Captor
An Argument Captor is like a net.
Instead of telling Mockito exactly what to expect, you say: "I don't know what object
the Service is going to pass to the Database. But when it does, throw a net over it,
capture it, and bring it back to me so I can inspect its internal variables using
JUnit."

Step 1: The Domain Object & Dependency
Step 2: The Class Under Test
Step 3: The Captor Test
/////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
Recap:
1. any(): Use when you don't care about the input parameter. (Also available:
   anyString(), anyObject()).
2. thenThrow(): The absolute best way to ensure your catch blocks actually work.
3. @Spy: Use when you need a real object to perform real logic, but you want to
   hijack just one specific method (like overriding a network call inside a real service).
4. mockStatic(): The nuclear option. Only use this for un-injectable static utilities like
   LocalDate.now() or UUID.randomUUID(). Always wrap it in a try() block to prevent test
   contamination.